diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 4963f09f6c8c6b7a328b101824a652fdfaf9edaa..fca6e284cf6100bc39acab601c16f15be6ad7f88 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -92,6 +92,76 @@ screen space?
 In the settings menu you can disable it. Then you'll only be able to use the
 hotkey (default shift-t) or the shortcut to show/hide the todo list.
 
+## Remote interface for other mods
+
+Other mods can add tasks to the shared list through the `todo_list` remote
+interface. The interface is registered while `control.lua` is loaded, so it is
+available from `on_init` onwards. Nothing else about the mod changes: without a
+`remote.call` the behaviour is exactly the same as before.
+
+Declare Todo List as an optional dependency (`? Todo-List >= 19.16.0`) so that
+your mod still loads when it is not installed, and guard every call:
+
+```lua
+if remote.interfaces["todo_list"] and remote.interfaces["todo_list"]["api_version"] then
+  local version = remote.call("todo_list", "api_version")
+  if version >= 1 then
+    local result = remote.call("todo_list", "add_task", {
+      player_index = player.index,
+      title = "Build 4 more red belt assemblers",
+      description = "Suggested by My Mod, iron gear throughput is the bottleneck.",
+      assignee = player.name,
+      add_to_top = true,
+    })
+
+    if result.created then
+      game.print("Created task " .. result.id)
+    else
+      game.print("Todo List rejected the task: " .. result.error)
+    end
+  end
+end
+```
+
+### `api_version() -> number`
+
+Returns the version of the remote contract, currently `1`. It is only bumped
+when an existing call changes in a breaking way, so `api_version() >= 1` is a
+safe guard. Adding new calls or new optional payload fields keeps it stable.
+
+### `add_task(payload) -> table`
+
+Creates one task in the open list, exactly like the add dialog does, and
+refreshes the list for every player.
+
+| field | type | required | notes |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `player_index` | number | yes | index of a valid player, becomes `created_by`/`updated_by` |
+| `title` | string | yes | at most 200 bytes after trimming |
+| `description` | string | no | at most 4000 bytes, `task` is accepted as an alias |
+| `assignee` | string | no | must be the name of an existing player |
+| `add_to_top` | boolean | no | defaults to `false` (append) |
+
+Control characters are stripped from all strings; tabs and line breaks are kept
+in the description. The limits are counted in bytes because Factorio does not
+ship Lua's `utf8` library.
+
+On success the call returns `{ created = true, id = <number> }`. The id is the
+regular task id, so it also shows up in exports. Refreshing the task list is
+best effort: once the task is in the list the call reports success even if a
+GUI update fails, so a caller can never see a failure for a task that exists.
+
+On rejection it returns `{ created = false, error = <string>, message = <string> }`
+instead of raising, so a bad payload can never crash the calling mod or the
+save. The `error` values are `invalid_payload`, `not_initialized`,
+`invalid_player_index`, `unknown_player`, `invalid_title`, `title_too_long`,
+`invalid_description`, `description_too_long`, `invalid_assignee`,
+`unknown_assignee`, `invalid_add_to_top` and `internal_error`. Treat unknown
+values as a generic failure.
+
+The interface deliberately exposes nothing else: there is no way to run
+arbitrary functions, to write to `storage` or to touch GUI elements.
+
 ## How to contribute?
 
 If you want to contribute feel free to create a [pull request](https://github.com/JonasJurczok/factorio-todo-list/pulls) (with new translations for example).
